---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Src from '../components/Src.astro';
const S = {
  ca: 'https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1950.6',
  or: 'https://www.oregonlegislature.gov/bills_laws/ors/ors090.html',
  mn: 'https://www.revisor.mn.gov/statutes/cite/504B.173',
  wa: 'https://app.leg.wa.gov/RCW/default.aspx?cite=59.18.257',
  md: 'https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Laws/StatuteText?article=grp&section=8-213&enactments=false',
};
const updated = '2026-11-24';
const title = 'Charged a Screening Fee but Never Screened: Refund Rules by State';
const description = 'If a landlord collects an application or background-check fee and never runs the check, or fills the unit first, when must the money come back? The refund rules and penalties in California, Oregon, Minnesota, Maryland and Washington, quoted from each statute.';
const schema = {"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article","headline":title,"description":description,"dateModified":updated};
---
<Base title={title} description={description} updated={updated} schema={schema}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p class="lede">A common complaint: an applicant pays a per-person "background check" fee, the unit goes to someone else, and the check was never run. Several states treat a screening fee as money for a specific purpose, so the part you didn't spend on screening goes back to the applicant. Here is what the statutes require, and what a landlord risks by keeping it.</p>

  <div class="note"><strong>The short version.</strong> <strong>California</strong> and <strong>Minnesota</strong> require returning any part of the fee not used, if no reference check or credit report was done <Src href={S.ca} label="Cal. Civ. 1950.6(e)" /> <Src href={S.mn} label="Minn. 504B.173 subd. 2(b)" />. <strong>Oregon</strong> requires a refund within 30 days if the unit is filled before screening, or the applicant withdraws in writing before any screening was ordered; failing to refund can cost twice the fee plus $250 <Src href={S.or} label="ORS 90.295(5), (6)(b)" />. <strong>Maryland</strong> requires returning non-deposit fees over $25, minus what was actually spent, within 15 days, for landlords with more than four units at one location <Src href={S.md} label="Real Prop. 8-213" />.</div>

  <h2>When does the fee have to be refunded?</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Refund rule</th><th>Deadline</th><th>Landlord's exposure</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>California</td>
        <td>"If the landlord or their agent does not perform a personal reference check or does not obtain a consumer credit report," any amount "not used for the purposes authorized" must be returned <Src href={S.ca} label="1950.6(e)" />. Under the fee process in (c)(2)(B), the entire fee goes back to every applicant not selected; under (c)(2)(A), applicants aren't charged until their application is actually considered, and fees collected from concurrent applications that aren't considered must be refunded <Src href={S.ca} label="1950.6(c)(2)" /></td>
        <td>Under (c)(2)(B): within 7 days of selecting a tenant or 30 days after the application, whichever is first. Under (c)(2)(A): within 7 days <Src href={S.ca} label="1950.6(c)(2)" /></td>
        <td>—</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Oregon</td>
        <td>Refund the screening charge if the landlord "fills the vacant dwelling unit before screening the applicant" or "has not conducted or ordered any screening of the applicant before the applicant withdraws the application in writing" <Src href={S.or} label="ORS 90.295(5)" /></td>
        <td>Within 30 days <Src href={S.or} label="90.295(5)" /></td>
        <td>Twice the screening charge plus $250 if the landlord doesn't screen "for any reason" and fails to refund within 30 days, or otherwise violates the section <Src href={S.or} label="90.295(6)(b)" /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Minnesota</td>
        <td>Return the fee if the applicant is rejected for a reason not in the landlord's written criteria, or if a prior applicant accepts the unit; if no reference check or credit or tenant screening report was done, return any unused amount <Src href={S.mn} label="504B.173 subd. 2" /></td>
        <td>—</td>
        <td>The screening fee plus a civil penalty of up to $100, filing costs and reasonable attorney fees <Src href={S.mn} label="subd. 4(a)" /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Maryland</td>
        <td>If non-deposit fees exceed $25, return them, keeping "only that portion of the fees actually expended for a credit check or other expenses arising out of the application." Doesn't apply to landlords offering four or fewer units on one parcel or at one location, or to seasonal or condominium rentals <Src href={S.md} label="Real Prop. 8-213(b), (c)" /></td>
        <td>Within 15 days after occupancy or after either side tells the other in writing that no tenancy will occur <Src href={S.md} label="8-213(b)(1)(ii)" /></td>
        <td>Twice the amount of the fees <Src href={S.md} label="8-213(b)(1)(i)" /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Washington</td>
        <td>No separate refund rule in this section. A landlord may charge for a screening report only after giving the required written notice, and a landlord who screens on their own may charge only "actual costs," capped at what local screening services customarily charge <Src href={S.wa} label="RCW 59.18.257(1)(b)" /></td>
        <td>—</td>
        <td>Up to $100 for violating subsection (1), plus court costs and attorneys' fees to the prevailing party <Src href={S.wa} label="59.18.257(3)" /></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>A dash means the statute doesn't set that item.</p>

  <h2>Can a landlord charge a fee when no unit is available?</h2>
  <p>Not in California, Oregon or Minnesota. California bars a screening fee when the landlord "know[s] or should have known that no rental unit is available at that time or will be available within a reasonable period of time" <Src href={S.ca} label="1950.6(c)(1)" />. Oregon has the same rule unless the applicant agrees otherwise in writing <Src href={S.or} label="ORS 90.295(4)" />, and Minnesota's is nearly identical <Src href={S.mn} label="504B.173 subd. 1(1)" />. Minnesota also bars the landlord from cashing or depositing a screening fee "until all prior applicants have either been screened and rejected, or offered the unit and declined" <Src href={S.mn} label="subd. 1(3)" />.</p>

  <h2>Does the applicant get proof of what was spent?</h2>
  <p>In California, the landlord must give a receipt that itemizes "the out-of-pocket expenses and time spent" on the applicant <Src href={S.ca} label="1950.6(d)" />. In Oregon, the landlord must give a receipt for the charge and, after a screening by a screening company or credit bureau, "confirmation of the screening, including a copy of a receipt from the company or agency" <Src href={S.or} label="ORS 90.295(1)(a)" />. Minnesota requires a written receipt on the applicant's request <Src href={S.mn} label="504B.173 subd. 1(2)" />.</p>

  <h2>How do you avoid the problem as a landlord?</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Don't collect fees from more applicants than you can actually consider. In California, Oregon and Minnesota, charging when no unit is or will reasonably be available is barred (see above).</li>
    <li>Screen in order. Minnesota requires it before you cash a fee <Src href={S.mn} label="subd. 1(3)" />, and one of California's two allowed fee processes considers completed applications in the order received <Src href={S.ca} label="1950.6(c)(2)(A)" />.</li>
    <li>Or let the screening service charge the applicant directly, so no fee passes through you. Our <a href="/tenant-screening-cost-calculator/">cost calculator</a> compares landlord-paid and applicant-paid setups.</li>
    <li>Keep the receipt from the screening company, since Oregon requires you to pass it on <Src href={S.or} label="ORS 90.295(1)(a)" />, and California requires an itemized receipt <Src href={S.ca} label="1950.6(d)" />.</li>
  </ol>

